2186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500302 #edb/ref 1071 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${3240}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500302 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c97d79e-a33f-5989-8625-5100ed2853d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500302 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=3\\,{240}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =3\\,{240}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{3\\,{240}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =18 \\\\ n & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=20$ sides." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1069 "q-23002" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1071 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${2700}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4852498b-dc1b-525e-82e2-305f7ebaf1ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500305 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=2\\,{700}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =2\\,{700}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{2\\,{700}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =15 \\\\ n & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=17$ sides." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4304" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/606a6eb139d8e5813f85c608824d8313a97ddaa465b7c05c89173247550a77ee.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ shown above, determine the measure of $∠C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97be54c9-28e3-5dbb-97cf-64bffd902d5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500330 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{30}^{∘})+{75}^{∘}+5x+(3x-{15}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 9x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 9x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =5x \\\\  & =5({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/72/72c03e75c41470f663cd78ed7ceb81a1c694c36adbb826c4b4d3f52346969be4.png)\n\nFind $m∠D$ in the pentagon $ABCDE$ above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1deaad5-98bb-5e5a-b9a0-860c7a294ceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500342 #edb/ref 1074 "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $m∠D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {135}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}+m∠D+{90}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {445}^{∘}+m∠D & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ m∠D & ={95}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74243" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1071 "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "be9eb345-ad38-54e4-ae93-ee7a03d3fd33" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500348 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)={360}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =2 \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=4$ sides (so it is a quadrilateral)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/23/23ae569de9ed72a8a2579c4a67eac48ae530aa89f19503e3b5d7ab8fb6ca6e80.png)\n\nIn the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown above, find $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d92c74e1-c89d-5cec-a7a1-0d82e6525e3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500365 #edb/ref 1074 "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {127}^{∘}+(2x)+(2x+{18}^{∘})+{110}^{∘}+(3x+{5}^{∘}) & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 7x+{260}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={280}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500263 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following could be the interior angles of a quadrilateral?\n\nI. ${41}^{∘}$, ${89}^{∘}$, ${100}^{∘}$, ${130}^{∘}$\n\nII. ${15}^{∘}$, ${119}^{∘}$, ${82}^{∘}$, ${131}^{∘}$\n\nIII. ${21}^{∘}$, ${74}^{∘}$, ${110}^{∘}$, ${155}^{∘}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d999c138-4be3-55d4-a8c5-6e1a2cbd965f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500366 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check this for the above three options:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{41}^{∘}+{89}^{∘}+{100}^{∘}+{130}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓ \\\\  &  \\\\ {15}^{∘}+{119}^{∘}+{82}^{∘}+{131}^{∘}={347}^{∘}\\,X \\\\  &  \\\\ {21}^{∘}+{74}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+{155}^{∘}={360}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only options I and III could be the angles of a quadrilateral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186500178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f9/f93eab26860fffd262ce4db8927c56d77ad7710b4b7da61e190126596e968d71.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2ab3104-7e40-5b36-8b8a-4688c13efdc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186500375 #edb/ref 1074 "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{90}^{∘})+(4x-{17}^{∘})+(3x+{3}^{∘})+{60}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 8x+{136}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 8x & ={224}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={28}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
